\begin{equation}
P_i^{\overline{K_i}}=Hardtanh(Y_{max,i}^{\overline{K_i}})
\setcounter{equation}{7}
\label{equation7}
\end{equation}
Hard tanh is a modified version of the tanh function that maps $Y_{max,i}^{\overline{K_i}}$ to the range (-1, 1).

Our purpose is to train the predicted value $P_i^{\overline{K_i}}$ of the background to tend to -1 to increase the gap between the foreground and the background, expand inter-class distance. Therefore, we use the mean square error between predicted $P_i^{\overline{K_i}}$ and pseudo target -1 as the  $i^{th}$ stage loss function $\mathcal{L}_{FD}^i$, as defined in equation (\ref{equation8}):
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{FD}^i={(P_i^{\overline{K_i}}+1)}^2
\setcounter{equation}{8}
\label{equation8}
\end{equation}


